{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Change UK law to recognise ownership of purchased digital products",
        "additional_details": "More products are now sold online instead of as physical copies. When someone buys a physical product, they own the copy they bought, even though they do not own the copyright. Digital products can instead be sold as a licence, meaning the buyer may not legally own what they paid for and the publisher may be able to withdraw access under its terms. We believe buying a digital product outright should give the buyer ownership of that copy.",
        "background": "Change UK consumer law to establish that consumers legally own the digital copies of digital products they purchase outright, rather than merely receiving a licence to use them.",
